/* Source-matched technical documentation; no solver behavior is changed. */
(() => {
'use strict';
window.SOLVER_TECH_GROUP = ({section,subsection,code,math,cite,table}) => {
const chain = section('tech-chain','Constructing a complete stabilizer chain',`
<p><strong>Purpose.</strong> Replace a search over whole configurations with reusable transporters in successively smaller permutation groups. The data remain exact permutations and legal move expressions. Compression comes from group structure, not a continuous relaxation or independent motion of stickers. Schreier–Sims supplies the group construction; the base, storage and word-cost choices below describe this implementation. ${cite('sims1971')} ${cite('jerrum1986')}</p>
${subsection('tech-chain-conventions','Composition, words and level invariants',`
<p>Let <i>N</i> be the permutation degree and Ω = {0,…,<i>N</i>−1} the address set. A permutation <i>p</i> is a length-<i>N</i> source-to-destination array: <code>p[q]</code> is the destination of <i>q</i>. The <i>m</i> legal primitive moves are <i>P</i><sub>0</sub>,…,<i>P</i><sub>m−1</sub>. Each array must contain every address exactly once. Inversion is <code>inv[p[q]] = q</code>; equality compares every entry.</p>
<p>Ordinary composition applies the rightmost map first: <code>COMPOSE(a,b)[q] = a[b[q]]</code>. The source function named <code>compose(a,b)</code> uses the opposite argument convention. Here that operation is called <code>THEN(a,b)[q] = b[a[q]]</code>: apply <i>a</i>, then <i>b</i>. A word <i>W</i> = [<i>j</i><sub>0</sub>,…,<i>j</i><sub>ℓ−1</sub>] lists playback order. Concatenating words <i>U</i> then <i>V</i> evaluates as <i>E</i>(<i>V</i>) ∘ <i>E</i>(<i>U</i>); <i>E</i>(<i>W</i>) = <i>P</i><sub>jℓ−1</sub>⋯<i>P</i><sub>j0</sub>.</p>
<p>Choose an ordered base <i>B</i> = (<i>b</i><sub>0</sub>,…,<i>b</i><sub>r−1</sub>). Let <i>H</i><sub>0</sub> be the group generated by the primitives, and <i>H</i><sub>i</sub> its subgroup fixing <i>b</i><sub>0</sub>,…,<i>b</i><sub>i−1</sub> pointwise. A full base makes <i>H</i><sub>r</sub> = {identity}. This implementation uses every address, so <i>r</i> = <i>N</i>; redundant levels have singleton orbits.</p>
${math('Descending pointwise stabilizers','<mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>}</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=".5em"/><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow>')}
<p>A strong-generator record holds its permutation, legal word reference and <em>level</em>: the first base index it moves. Pool <i>S</i><sub>i</sub> consists of records with level ≥ <i>i</i>. At completion it generates exactly <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>. During construction it may generate only a subgroup, so a missing orbit entry is not yet an impossibility proof.</p>
<p>Level <i>i</i> stores orbit <i>O</i><sub>i</sub> = {<i>h</i>(<i>b</i><sub>i</sub>): <i>h</i> ∈ <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>} and transporter <i>T</i><sub>i</sub>[<i>q</i>] for each orbit address. That transporter fixes earlier base points and sends <i>b</i><sub>i</sub> to <i>q</i>; the base point’s entry is identity. Each orbit has at most <i>N</i> addresses, not a list of whole puzzle states.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-chain-base','Choosing the full-solve base',`
<p>Group addresses by their orbits under primitive moves: scan starting addresses in ascending order, run address BFS from each unseen start, sort the resulting orbit numerically and append it. For a pose-expanded model, stable-partition the result so occupied home addresses come first. Empty home addresses remain afterward to certify the full permutation group, although Auto Solve need not restore them.</p>
${code('CHOOSE-BASE',`seen ← empty set; B ← []
for b = 0,…,N−1:
    if b in seen: continue
    queue ← [b]; add b to seen
    for each x in queue, including later appended addresses:
        for each primitive P:
            y ← P[x]
            if y not in seen: add y to seen; append y to queue
    append sorted(queue) to B
if model requires occupied-state tracking:
    B ← [b in B initially occupied] ++ [b in B initially empty]
require B is a permutation of 0,…,N−1
return B`)}
<p>An arbitrary held subset generally requires a different stabilizer. The manual-request chapter specifies its held-first base and the eligibility calculation’s base change. One full-solve cache is not automatically a cache for every possible held subset.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-chain-words','Signed expression DAG: constructing and expanding legal words',`
<p>A directed acyclic graph (DAG) stores reusable expressions. Reference 0 denotes identity; a positive reference names a node; its negative denotes the inverse. A leaf stores a primitive move index. An internal node <code>(a,b)</code> executes signed reference <code>a</code>, then <code>b</code>, evaluating as <code>THEN(E(a),E(b))</code>. Inversion needs no copied tree: <code>−(a,b)</code> executes <code>(−b,−a)</code>.</p>
<p><code>JOIN</code> removes identities and the exact pair <code>a,−a</code>; otherwise it appends a node. It does not perform generic structural hash-consing. Reuse comes from referring to earlier witnesses. Raw lengths add and saturate at JavaScript’s maximum safe integer; they can exceed the flattened length after cancellations.</p>
${code('WORDS helpers',`JOIN(a,b):
    if a = 0: return b
    if b = 0: return a
    if a = −b: return 0
    check DAG-node and estimated-memory limits
    append node(a,b, min(MAX_SAFE_INTEGER,len(a)+len(b)))
    return its positive reference

LIST(parts):
    ref ← 0
    for part in parts: ref ← JOIN(ref,part)
    return ref

FLATTEN(ref):
    stack ← [ref]; output ← []; visits ← 0
    while stack not empty:
        increment visits; check work/time limits
        r ← pop(stack)
        if r = 0: continue
        if abs(r) is a leaf for primitive j:
            if r > 0: PUSH-REDUCED(output,j)
            else if inverse primitive exists:
                PUSH-REDUCED(output,inverseIndex[j])
            else:
                compute finite order o of P[j] by repeated composition
                emit o−1 copies of j through PUSH-REDUCED
            check output-length limit
        else let (a,b) be that node:
            if r > 0: push b, then a onto stack
            else:     push −a, then −b onto stack
    return output

PUSH-REDUCED(output,j):
    if final output move has inverseIndex equal to j: pop it
    else: append j`)}
<p>The LIFO push order is essential: a negative concatenation executes the inverse of the last child first. If no inverse primitive exists, finite order <i>o</i> gives <i>P</i><sup>−1</sup> = <i>P</i><sup>o−1</sup>; the implementation bounds this fallback. <code>FLATTEN</code> caps visits at <code>max(10,000,100 × maxOutputMoves)</code>, checks time every 1,024 visits and limits output throughout. A small DAG can encode an enormous word. Expansion limits return <strong>unknown</strong>, never a truncated purported solution.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-chain-orbits','Orbit transporters: unweighted BFS and weighted Dijkstra',`
<p>If a transporter reaches <i>x</i>, following generator <i>s</i> reaches <i>y</i> = <i>s</i>[<i>x</i>] with permutation <code>THEN(T[x],s)</code> and word <code>JOIN(word[x],word[s])</code>. Earlier base points remain fixed. Unweighted FIFO BFS minimizes the number of current generator records on this address path, not primitive turns inside their words.</p>
<p>The weighted version gives edge <i>s</i> cost <code>len(word[s])</code> and uses Dijkstra’s algorithm. It selects the next vertex by scanning all <i>N</i> addresses; no heap is implicit. It materializes the transporter when the vertex is finalized. These costs are raw DAG lengths, not fully canceled concatenation lengths. The selected generator pool and cross-word cancellations therefore prevent a global shortestness claim.</p>
${code('ORBITS(S,B,weighted)',`levels ← []
for i = 0,…,r−1:
    Si ← generators in S with firstMovedBaseIndex ≥ i
    b ← B[i]; T ← fresh length-N array of absent entries; order ← []
    T[b] ← {permutation:I,word:0}
    if not weighted:
        queue ← [b]
        for each x in queue:
            append x to order
            for s in Si:
                y ← s.permutation[x]
                if T[y] absent:
                    T[y] ← {THEN(T[x].permutation,s.permutation),
                            JOIN(T[x].word,s.word)}
                    append y to queue
    else:
        dist[0…N−1] ← infinity; done[0…N−1] ← false
        parent ← fresh length-N array; dist[b] ← 0
        repeat:
            x ← unfinished address with smallest finite dist
            if no such x: break
            mark x done; append x to order
            if x ≠ b:
                (v,s) ← parent[x]
                T[x] ← {THEN(T[v].permutation,s.permutation),
                        JOIN(T[v].word,s.word)}
            for s in Si:
                y ← s.permutation[x]; cost ← dist[x]+len(s.word)
                if cost < dist[y]:
                    dist[y] ← cost; parent[y] ← (x,s)
    levels[i] ← {base:b,generators:Si,representatives:T,order}
return levels`)}
<p>Rebuilds reuse levels deeper than the new generator’s first moved base index: it cannot belong to those deeper pools. Affected shallower levels are rebuilt. Inverse transporter arrays are created lazily when sifting needs them.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-chain-closure','Schreier residuals, sifting and exact completion',`
<p>For each orbit address <i>x</i> and current generator <i>s</i>, set <i>y</i> = <i>s</i>[<i>x</i>]. Execute <i>T</i>[<i>x</i>], then <i>s</i>, then <i>T</i>[<i>y</i>]<sup>−1</sup>. This Schreier permutation fixes the current base point as well as earlier ones:</p>
${math('A Schreier permutation fixes the current base point','<mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∘</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=".5em"/><mi>h</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow>')}
<p><em>Sifting</em> removes a candidate’s action on successive base points. If <i>h</i>(<i>b</i><sub>k</sub>) = <i>x</i>, execute <i>h</i> then <i>T</i><sub>k</sub>[<i>x</i>]<sup>−1</sup>; the residual now fixes that point. A missing transporter identifies the first unresolved level. Append the same inverse word to the candidate’s expression so it remains a witnessed legal product.</p>
<p>This is also the factorization itself. Starting with <i>h</i><sub>0</sub> = <i>g</i>, choose <i>x</i><sub>i</sub> = <i>h</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>b</i><sub>i</sub>) and set <i>h</i><sub>i+1</sub> = <i>T</i><sub>i</sub>[<i>x</i><sub>i</sub>]<sup>−1</sup> ∘ <i>h</i><sub>i</sub>. If the final residual is identity, then <i>g</i> = <i>T</i><sub>0</sub>[<i>x</i><sub>0</sub>] ∘ <i>T</i><sub>1</sub>[<i>x</i><sub>1</sub>] ∘ ⋯ ∘ <i>T</i><sub>r−1</sub>[<i>x</i><sub>r−1</sub>]. The destinations depend on earlier residuals; they are not simply the original images <i>g</i>(<i>b</i><sub>i</sub>). To undo <i>g</i>, play the inverse transporter words chronologically from level 0 upward.</p>
${code('SIFT and BUILD-CHAIN',`SIFT(h,start,levels,parts):
    for k = start,…,r−1:
        x ← h[B[k]]
        if x = B[k]: continue
        if levels[k].T[x] absent: return (h,k,parts)
        t ← levels[k].T[x]
        h ← THEN(h,INVERSE(t.permutation))
        append −t.word to parts
    return (h,r,parts)

BUILD-CHAIN(primitives,B,weighted):
    S ← unique nonidentity primitives with leaf words and levels
    known ← keys of their complete permutation arrays
    dirty ← r−1; levels ← absent
    repeat:
        check time, generator-count and memory limits
        rebuild levels 0…dirty with ORBITS; reuse deeper levels
        added ← false
        for i = dirty,…,0, stopping immediately when added:
            for x in levels[i].order:
                for s in levels[i].generators:
                    check Schreier-test budget
                    y ← s.permutation[x]
                    tx ← levels[i].T[x]; ty ← levels[i].T[y]
                    h ← THEN(THEN(tx.permutation,s.permutation),
                             INVERSE(ty.permutation))
                    if h = I: continue
                    (h,k,parts) ← SIFT(h,i+1,levels,
                                      [tx.word,s.word,−ty.word])
                    if h = I: continue
                    require key(h) not in known
                    w ← LIST(parts)
                    if weighted: optionally normalize w as below
                    append {h,w,firstMovedBaseIndex(h)} to S
                    add key(h) to known
                    dirty ← k; added ← true
                    break all three loops
        if not added: return complete chain(S,B,levels,WORDS)`)}
<p>The weighted builder normalizes a new witness only when raw length ≤ 2,000: flatten, replace adjacent primitive pairs equal to identity or one primitive, then rebuild the DAG word. Optionally it seeds nonidentity commutators <code>[a,b,a⁻¹,b⁻¹]</code>, ordered by moved-address count; this option is off unless requested. Unweighted construction omits these word-cost choices.</p>
<p><strong>Completion proof.</strong> Schreier’s lemma says these residuals generate the stabilizer of the current base point. When every residual reduces to identity through deeper tables, the required deeper stabilizers are represented. Induct down the full base to certify every orbit and subgroup, with no unresolved nonidentity residual. A limit before that fixed point is not a complete chain.</p>
<p>The certified group order is <i>|H</i><sub>0</sub><i>|</i> = ∏<sub>i</sub><i>|O</i><sub>i</sub><i>|</i>. Orbit sizes give that number without listing the group elements. The independent cache audit also recomputes a chain and compares its order with the product of cached orbit sizes after verifying every stored witness and prefix-fixing property. The JSON flag <code>complete:true</code> alone is not a mathematical certificate.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-chain-cost','Dimensions, computational cost and actual budgets',`
<p>Let <i>r</i><sub>i</sub> = <i>|O</i><sub>i</sub><i>|</i>, <i>s</i><sub>i</sub> = <i>|S</i><sub>i</sub><i>|</i>, <i>R</i> = ∑<sub>i</sub><i>r</i><sub>i</sub>, <i>g</i> be total strong-generator count, and <i>W</i> the DAG node count. Each array uses <i>N</i> 32-bit integers. Composition, inversion, identity checks and full-array keys cost O(<i>N</i>); a single address lookup costs O(1).</p>
${table(['Operation or storage','Cost of this implementation'],[
['One orbit rebuild','O(rg + ∑<sub>i</sub>(r<sub>i</sub>s<sub>i</sub> + Nr<sub>i</sub>)). This includes filtering generator pools, address edges, full transporter arrays, and, in weighted mode, linear priority scans.'],
['One Schreier candidate and sift','At most O(N(r−i+1)) array work starting at level i, plus word bookkeeping. Multiply by candidates actually tested across rebuilds.'],
['Live chain','O(N(m+g+R)+W), plus indexing and JavaScript object overhead. Lazy inverse arrays can nearly double relevant permutation storage.'],
['Serialized cache','O(N(R−r)+W+r) numeric entries: full nonidentity transporter arrays, shared words and base/level metadata.'],
['Full-base worst case','r=N, r<sub>i</sub>≤N−i, hence R≤N(N+1)/2. Explicit transporter storage can be cubic in N; it is not automatically linear.']])}
<p>These costs use permutation degree, not configuration count. They do not guarantee small worst-case runtime for this bounded implementation. Generator growth, closure passes and expanded words remain practical constraints.</p>
<p>The group context defaults to 2 seconds, 2,000 strong generators, 300,000 DAG nodes, 3,000,000 Schreier tests, 100,000 accumulated unweighted orbit states, 20,000 output moves and estimated 64 MiB storage. Full precomputation overrides these to 30 seconds, 5,000 generators, 500,000 DAG nodes, 20,000,000 Schreier tests, 100,000 representative output moves and estimated 160 MiB. Callers may override them. The offline batch uses 60 seconds for most puzzles; 7×7×7 and 8×8×8 use the unweighted builder, 150 seconds and estimated 192 MiB.</p>
<p>Storage checks are accounting estimates, not measured browser heap use. Unweighted construction checks accumulated orbit states and estimated allocations; weighted construction also estimates the chain footprint after rebuilding. Time checks are cooperative, not hard real-time deadlines. A guard produces <code>unknown</code>, with reasons including <code>time_limit</code>, <code>storage_limit</code>, <code>strong_generator_limit</code>, <code>word_dag_limit</code>, <code>schreier_test_limit</code>, <code>orbit_storage_limit</code> or expansion/output limits.</p>
`)}
`, 'Build exact address orbits and legal transporter words until every Schreier residual reduces to identity.');

const full = section('tech-full','Auto Solve: constructive placements with color-route shortening',`
<p><strong>Auto Solve is constructive placement, not reverse playback.</strong> It first builds the identity-correct baseline specified below. For explicitly supported color-only goals it then shortens that legal route in color space and rebuilds secured stage endpoints. Shape and picture goals retain the exact baseline. A stage is a placement or cycle, not necessarily an isolated two-way swap.</p>
${subsection('tech-full-input','Input contract, occupied poses and exact goal',`
<p>Let <i>S</i> be the number of real sticker identities. The objects <code>homeBySticker[id]</code> and <code>currentBySticker[id]</code> map identical sets of <i>S</i> keys to home and current addresses. They must be nonempty, injective and in range; a declared model <code>stickerCount</code> must equal <i>S</i>. Fixed-slot models usually have <i>S</i> = <i>N</i>; pose-expanded models can have <i>S</i> &lt; <i>N</i>. Empty poses receive no dummy sticker identities.</p>
<p>Construct <code>atHome[h] = currentBySticker[id]</code> for the identity with <code>homeBySticker[id] = h</code>. Thus the value is the present location of the sticker whose target is <i>h</i>. Occupied home addresses must be exactly the first <i>S</i> cache-base entries. Solve only this prefix, leaving permutations of empty addresses unconstrained.</p>
<p>The baseline goal restores <em>identity</em>, not just displayed color. Equal-colored stickers can look solved while remaining permuted. The second pass below relaxes this only when <code>colorRelaxationSafe=true</code>, the domain is fully occupied, no expanded-pose requirement applies, and every physical identity has a validated color label. No whole-puzzle-rotation equivalence is introduced. An already-solved supported color goal returns an independently verified zero-move plan; other models retain the identity test.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-full-stage','The complete placement algorithm',`
<p>If the locked prefix ends before <i>b</i><sub>i</sub> and its target sticker is at <i>q</i>, transporter <i>T</i><sub>i</sub>[<i>q</i>] sends <i>b</i><sub>i</sub> to <i>q</i>. Execute its inverse to bring the sticker home. It lies in <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>, preserving earlier homes at the stage endpoint. Individual turns inside the stage may disturb them.</p>
${code('SOLVE-FULL',`validate identity maps
if every current[id] = home[id]: return verified empty solution
load or construct cache; validate schema, degree, signature, completeness
atHome ← map home[id] ↦ current[id]
B ← cache.base restricted to occupied home addresses
require these are the first S cache.base entries
locked ← []; stages ← []; allMoves ← []; i ← 0
while i < S and atHome[B[i]] = B[i]:
    append B[i] to locked; i ← i+1
initialProtectedNodes ← copy(locked)

while i < S:
    check planning deadline
    target ← B[i]; source ← atHome[target]
    require cache.levels[i].base = target
    entry ← level-i transporter whose destination = source
    if entry absent:
        return impossible(state_outside_legal_stabilizer_orbit)
    sequence ← FLATTEN(−entry.word)
    if total output exceeds budget: return unknown(output_move_limit)
    if explicit shorten option is on and time remains:
        shorten sequence boundedly, preserving its full permutation
    p ← I
    for j in sequence: check time; p ← THEN(p,P[j])
    if p[source] ≠ target or some locked h has p[h] ≠ h:
        return unknown(internal_stage_verification_failed)
    heldBefore ← copy(locked)
    for every home h: atHome[h] ← p[atHome[h]]
    newlySolved ← []
    do:
        append B[i] to newlySolved and locked; i ← i+1
    while i < S and atHome[B[i]] = B[i]
    append {sequence,heldBefore,newlySolved,target,source} to stages
    append sequence to allMoves

if some occupied home h has atHome[h] ≠ h:
    return unknown(internal_final_verification_failed)
return solved(stages,allMoves,initialProtectedNodes,locked,
              verified=true,optimal=false)`)}
<p>Only the solved <em>prefix</em> is locked. A later sticker already at home can move until its level is reached. A stage may also solve several consecutive addresses, locking them together. Returned <code>protectedNodes</code>, <code>newlySolvedNodes</code>, <code>targetNode</code> and <code>sourceNode</code> records drive animation highlights and endpoint checks. Playback must recheck the live engine; a witness planned for one snapshot cannot safely be applied to a changed state.</p>
<p>Induction proves correctness: the initial prefix is solved; each inverse transporter extends it while fixing previous endpoints; at most <i>S</i> stages restore every occupied home. For a reachable state the residual after earlier corrections lies in <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>, so its required source is in <i>O</i><sub>i</sub>. A missing entry in a complete, validated chain therefore proves the labeled arrangement unreachable under this move model. It does not prove a different color-only target impossible. When the requested goal is color-based, this branch returns <code>unknown</code> with <code>color_goal_identity_baseline_unreachable</code>; the certified impossibility status applies only to the identity goal.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-full-status','Results, cost and solution-length guarantees',`
${table(['Result','Meaning'],[
['<code>solved</code>','All stage endpoints and final identity mapping verify. The empty solution has <code>optimal:true</code>; constructive nonempty solutions have <code>optimal:false</code>.'],
['<code>impossible</code>','Source outside a certified orbit: <code>state_outside_legal_stabilizer_orbit</code>, proof <code>complete_stabilizer_chain</code>.'],
['<code>unknown</code>','Planning, storage, expansion or output budget; unavailable cache; or failed internal stage/final verification. None proves mathematical impossibility.'],
['<code>invalid</code>','Missing or mismatched identity keys, noninjective addresses, wrong sticker count, incompatible cache/signature/base or malformed data.']])}
<p><code>solveFull</code> defaults to 30 seconds and 20,000 moves; callers may override them. Without a supplied cache it can precompute within remaining time; normal browser use loads the packaged cache. This function’s stage shortening is opt-in, only for sequences longer than ten moves, using at most 80 ms and 4 MiB. The output cap is checked before optional shortening, so an overlong raw stage can return unknown even when a shorter equivalent exists.</p>
<p>If <i>ℓ</i><sub>i,q</sub> is a representative’s raw primitive-word length, ∑<sub>i</sub> max<sub>q</sub><i>ℓ</i><sub>i,q</sub> bounds the unreduced constructed output over legal states. Skipped levels and cancellation can shorten it. This is neither the puzzle’s diameter nor a guarantee that every solve fits the browser’s output budget.</p>
<p>With <i>L</i> emitted moves, <i>V</i> DAG expansion visits, <i>R</i> cached orbit entries, <i>W</i> word nodes and <i>S</i> sticker identities, planning once the cache is loaded, excluding precomputation and optional shortening, is O(<i>mN+W+R+V+NL+S</i><sup>2</sup>): compiling primitive arrays, decoding word lengths, linear entry lookups, expansion, full-array replay and identity-position updates. Parsing the serialized cache separately costs its full size, including transporter arrays. No <i>m</i><sup>L</sup> configuration tree is constructed.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-full-color','Color quotient: remove loops, bridge the witness, rebuild protected progress',`
<p>For a safe color-only model, encode the full visible state as a length-N string C. A primitive p acts by C′[p[x]]=C[x] for every source x. This action is deterministic on colors: two labeled states with the same C remain color-equal after any common legal suffix. Consequently a segment returning to the same C can be deleted without invalidating the remaining color solution. This never assumes arbitrary equal-colored sticker swaps are independently legal; every retained or inserted turn is an original legal move.</p>
${code('Bounded witnessed-path simplification',`COLOR-OPTIMIZE(baseline, initialColors, goalColors, budget):
  if model is not explicitly color-safe: return baseline
  if initialColors=goalColors: return verified empty color plan
  record initial state and its index in a state->index map
  mark the baseline's cumulative stage endpoints
  for each primitive move in baseline order:
    advance the current full color string by that move
    if the string is already retained at index j:
      erase records after j and remove their map entries
      retain any stage-end flag at the repeated state
    else:
      append (state, incoming move, original-stage-end flag)
    if goalColors is reached: stop scanning
    if scan time or state-memory guard expires: return baseline
  require the last retained string equals goalColors

  start at retained index 0
  while not at final index:
    next := following retained index; move := its incoming move
    while shortcut-check budget permits, test each legal primitive:
      look up its resulting color string in the retained-state map
      if it reaches an index later than next: keep that index and move
    append the chosen legal edge; advance to chosen index
  retain surviving original stage flags; always flag final state
  rebuild stages and validate as below; otherwise return baseline`)}
<p>The bridge pass is greedy: it selects the farthest later retained state reachable by one tested legal move. It does not search arbitrary configurations, and it does not prove an optimal route even within all possible shortcuts. The nominal optimizer budget is 250 ms, at most 12,000 primitive bridge checks, and 16 MiB estimated state-record storage. Bridge checks stop at 70% of the time allowance to reserve time for reconstruction and verification. The existing overall solve deadline also applies. State capacity is floor(bytes/(2N+160)), with a minimum of two records. If the mandatory loop scan cannot finish within its guard, keep the independently valid baseline.</p>
${code('Rebuild secured colors at surviving boundaries',`boundaries := initial state plus surviving stage-end states
suffixMask := all N bits set
for boundary k from last to first:
  correctMask[n] := (boundary[k].color[n] == goalColor[n])
  suffixMask := suffixMask AND correctMask
  secure[k] := copy(suffixMask)
locked := secure[0]; initialProtectedNodes := nodes(locked)
from := initial boundary
for each later boundary k:
  newly := secure[k] minus locked
  if newly is empty: continue // merge this interval into a later stage
  word := all retained legal edges from from through k
  g := independently compose word
  target := a newly-secured node incorrect at from, if one exists
  source := inverse(g)[target]
  require source's starting color matches target's goal color
  require every locked and newly-secured location is correct at k
  emit {word,source,target,protected:locked,newlySolved:newly}
  locked := locked union newly; from := k
require final boundary reached and all N locations secured
require moves <= baseline moves and stages <= baseline stages
independently replay the full word and verify the color goal`)}
<p>The suffix intersection means a node becomes secured only when its goal color is correct at this boundary and every future retained boundary. This permits a same-color replacement while guaranteeing every displayed hold at completed stage endpoints. Intermediate turns may still disturb it. Boundaries with no newly secured colors merge into a later stage, removing pauses devoted only to identity distinctions. No promise is made that an accepted stage fixes held sticker identities.</p>
<p>Color plans carry <code>goalSemantics:'colors'</code> and method <code>color_quotient_witness_shortcuts</code>; legacy plans have the identity interpretation. The independent verifier uses the declared goal, rejects color plans for unsafe models, replays every primitive, checks the source-to-target image, and checks all protected/new colors after each stage. It still tracks complete sticker identities to detect stale execution and wrong engine moves.</p>
<p>For baseline length L, Q bridge checks and B retained boundaries, the additional work is O(N(L+Q+B)), plus legal-word replay. Stored color records require O(NL) entries in the uncapped case but are bounded by the byte guard here; masks require O(NB). Accepted optimization never increases primitive count or stage count. It adds bounded planning work, so shorter playback is not a claim that every solve uses less CPU. Global shortest length and improvement on every scramble are not guaranteed.</p>
`)}
`, 'Apply inverse transporters to extend a verified solved prefix, using the current state rather than the move journal.');

const shorten = section('tech-shorten','Shorter witnesses, serialized caches and independent validation',`
<p>Correct and shortest factorization are separate tasks. Word rewrites preserve the complete permutation. Cache refinement may instead choose a different permutation with the same required transporter action. Both reduce playback while retaining their stated guarantees; neither certifies globally shortest solutions. Short-word factorization has its own literature; our bounded cache optimizer is an implementation choice, not a claim to reproduce every detail of Minkwitz’s method. ${cite('minkwitz1998')}</p>
${subsection('tech-color-catalog','Short legal-operation catalog: bounded precomputation for every puzzle',`
<p>A separate catalog stores short words with at least one actual two-cycle. It supplements, and does not replace, the certified complete stabilizer chain. Each operation stores a primitive-index word and its complete sparse support as alternating source,destination entries; omitted sources are fixed. Color labels are not precomputed because a scramble changes which labels occupy each address.</p>
${code('COMPILE-COLOR-CATALOG',`require the primitive move alphabet is inverse-closed
known := map from complete permutation to shortest encountered word
for each primitive a: CONSIDER([a])
for each ordered primitive pair a,b:
  CONSIDER([a,b])
  CONSIDER([a,b,a^-1])
  CONSIDER([a,b,a^-1,b^-1])
  CONSIDER([a,b,a,b])
  CONSIDER([a,b,a,b,a,b])
if no two-cycle operation was found and move count <= 16:
  enumerate words to depth 4, excluding immediate inverse pairs
  CONSIDER each word of length 3 or 4

CONSIDER(word):
  g := chronological primitive replay
  if no a<b satisfies g[a]=b and g[b]=a: discard
  retain shortest encountered word for exactly this permutation

prioritize one-move words, then increasing 2*wordLength+movedNodeCount
retain at most 10,000 operations and a 2 MiB per-model JSON budget
sort retained operations by wordLength, then movedNodeCount
independently replay every word and compare its entire sparse support
write signature-bound catalog and verification report`)}
<p>For N addresses and m primitives, the algebraic word families require O(m²N) array work, before sorting and bounded retention. The optional depth-four enumeration is deliberately limited to m≤16 and does not grow with scramble length. Deduplication is by the complete resulting permutation, not color pattern or support size. Tie-breaking may choose any shortest encountered legal word; the catalog is not exhaustive over all words up to six moves.</p>
<p>Regenerate with <code>node solver-backend/tools/precompute-color.cjs</code>, verify with <code>node solver-backend/tools/verify-color-precompute.cjs</code>, then rebuild the browser bundle. Schema version 1 uses <code>kind:'color-macros'</code>, <code>modelId</code>, <code>modelSignature</code>, <code>degree</code>, and <code>operations:[{word,support}]</code>. Every model signature must match the primitive permutations. The original short-word build imposes a 20 MiB aggregate cap; the verified conjugacy extension below allows at most 24 MiB for the final catalogs. Only the selected puzzle's catalog is loaded.</p>
<p>The final 34-model catalog contains 70,014 independently replayed operations, totals 15,506,236 raw JSON bytes, and has a longest retained word of 25 moves. This combines the initial short-word families, exact double-swap witnesses, and the bounded conjugacy extension below. These are legal witnesses available for many possible color states, not stored configurations. A missing operation cannot prove impossibility.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-catalog-conjugacy','Expand useful operations by sparse conjugacy',`
<p>An operation useful at one set of addresses can be moved to a different set by a legal setup. Let W be its source-to-destination permutation and Q one legal primitive. The matrix product C = Q W Q⁻¹ maps Q(s) to Q(t) whenever W maps s to t. Its chronological word is <code>[Q⁻¹, word(W), Q]</code>. Consequently the cycle structure is unchanged, and the candidate support can be computed by relabeling only the moved source/destination pairs. The order of the inverse is essential.</p>
${code('EXPAND-SPARSE-CONJUGATES',`require an inverse primitive index for every allowed move
original := map from canonical sparse support to existing operation
minimumSupport := smallest number of support integers in original
seedPool := operations with supportLength <= max(minimumSupport,24)
            and wordLength <= 40 - 2*2
sort seedPool by wordLength, then supportLength; keep at most 128
queue := these seeds with depth 0; discovered := empty support map
for each queued operation W, while expansionCount < 6000:
  if W.depth == 2: skip
  for each primitive Q, while expansionCount < 6000:
    support := sort pairs (Q[s],Q[t]) for every stored pair (s,t) in W
    word := [inverseIndex(Q)] ++ W.word ++ [index(Q)]
    skip if original or discovered has this same support at no greater cost
    retain candidate at depth W.depth+1
    enqueue it only if its depth < 2
rank candidates by supportLength + 2*wordLength, then stable tie-breaks
for each candidate within 2000 new words and 256 KiB net added bytes:
  independently replay all legal primitives
  assert replay's full sparse support equals candidate.support
  if same support already stored: replace only with a shorter word
  else append this verified new witness
sort final operations by wordLength, supportLength and support key
verify every resulting catalog; enforce 24 MiB aggregate serialized size`)}
<p>The two depth limits count setup layers around an already-known operation, not scramble depth. If s support pairs move, one relabeling takes O(s) array lookups plus O(s log s) canonical sorting. Candidate full-word replay costs O(NL) for N model addresses and L primitive moves. Queue expansion, retention and bytes are explicitly capped. Nothing enumerates all puzzle configurations or all possible held sets. Old witnesses are retained unless an exactly equal permutation has a shorter verified expression.</p>
<p>This extension added 36,299 operations and shortened 284 existing expressions. It does not make the catalog exhaustive, and a catalog miss remains unknown. Run <code>node solver-backend/tools/expand-color-conjugates.cjs</code> after the short-word and exact-double-swap precomputations; then verify catalogs and rebuild the browser bundle.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-catalog-index','Index witnesses by their actual exchanged pair',`
<p>Manual requests select only two addresses. Scanning operations that do not exchange those addresses wastes work. Build an index once for each immutable loaded catalog: the unordered pair key is min(a,b)·N + max(a,b). An operation may appear under several keys because it may contain several disjoint two-cycles.</p>
${code('BUILD-PAIR-INDEX and LOOKUP',`byPair := empty map
for each operation op:
  map := its sparse source-to-destination support
  reject malformed support as an index entry
  for each source a and destination b in map:
    if a < b and map[b] == a:
      append op to byPair[a*N+b]
for each pair list: sort by wordLength, then supportLength
memoize index for this immutable catalog object and operation array

LOOKUP(a,b,F,colors):
  candidates := byPair[min(a,b)*N+max(a,b)] or empty list
  for op in candidates, respecting the request budget:
    test colors arriving at every active held destination
    skip if any active hold fails
    replay op.word against the authoritative primitive permutations
    verify the complete support, both selected identities and every hold
    return first valid witness, or continue after an invalid declaration
  return NO_CATALOG_WITNESS // not an impossibility proof`)}
<p>If S is total sparse-support size and kₚ is the number of operations indexed for pair p, construction costs O(S + ∑ₚ kₚ log kₚ), assuming constant-time hash operations. Storage is proportional to the total indexed two-cycles plus pair-map entries. A later request tests only k₍ₐ,ᵦ₎ candidates, rather than every catalog operation. The first fully verified acceptable candidate has minimum stored word length in this list, not necessarily minimum legal distance. A time-limited scan has no such global claim. Progressive sessions retain the index and their candidate cursor; changing the request invalidates the session.</p>
<p>The implementation charges an estimate of 16 bytes per pair-to-operation entry plus 96 bytes per pair-map entry. This is a guard, not a browser heap measurement. A full permutation declaration never becomes trusted merely because it was indexed: replay and endpoint verification remain required. The index is <code>src/macro-index.cjs</code>; it is built lazily, not downloaded as a separate table.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-shorten-local','Local equal-permutation rewrites',`
<p>Three reductions are distinct. DAG expansion cancels adjacent inverse primitives. Weighted-chain normalization builds an <i>m</i> × <i>m</i> table and replaces adjacent pairs equal to identity or one available primitive, in a left-to-right stack pass. The runtime shortener examines longer windows using a dictionary of identity, all one-move products and all two-move products.</p>
${code('SHORTEN(model,word)',`dictionary ← {key(I): []}
insert primitive permutations with one-move witnesses unless present
for a = 0,…,m−1:
    for b = 0,…,m−1:
        if time or byte budget exceeded: return original word, limited=true
        k ← key(THEN(P[a],P[b]))
        if k absent: dictionary[k] ← [a,b]
out ← copy(word)
for at most two passes:
    any ← false
    for start = 0,…,length(out)−1:
        if deadline reached: return current out, limited=true
        p ← I; best ← absent
        for length = 1,…,6, while window remains in out:
            p ← THEN(p,P[out[start+length−1]])
            replacement ← dictionary[key(p)]
            if replacement exists and is shorter than length:
                best ← (length,replacement)
        if best exists:
            replace that window; any ← true
            restart scan up to three positions before start
    if not any: break
return out, limited=false`)}
<p>Dictionary insertion keeps the first, shortest-length witness encountered. Replacement requires equality of the complete length-<i>N</i> permutation, not just selected nodes or colors. Endpoint behavior on every address is therefore unchanged, but intermediate states may differ; endpoint rewrites are not automatically valid for a requirement to hold nodes after every primitive turn.</p>
<p>Defaults are 100 ms and estimated 8 MiB. Dictionary construction costs O(<i>m</i><sup>2</sup><i>N</i>) time and at most O(<i>m</i><sup>2</sup><i>N</i>) key storage. Windows use at most six compositions; successful replacements strictly shorten the word. A limit returns the original or partly shortened <em>valid</em> word. This differs from exhausting resources before finding any witness, which returns unknown.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-shorten-offline','Offline representative improvement and its acceptance gate',`
<p>The optimizer preserves the certified base and every orbit destination. It substitutes a shorter legal prefix-fixing representative for the same destination when found. It never removes an entry because no short replacement was found.</p>
<p>The replacement can be a <em>different full permutation</em>: only membership in the earlier-point stabilizer and the image of the current base point must agree. It may rearrange other, unprotected addresses differently. This is why representative refinement is more flexible than replacing a substring of an already chosen word.</p>
<p>First flatten original representatives only when raw length ≤ 1,500; otherwise retain the original DAG witness. Reduce flattened words with exact commutation and pair-product tables: move a new primitive left across commuting suffix moves until it can combine with an earlier move, then replay the displaced suffix. Every rewrite is an identity of full permutations.</p>
${code('COMMUTING-REDUCE and bounded generator insertion',`For primitives a,b, precompute:
    combine[a,b] ← identity marker, primitive index, or absent,
        according to the full permutation THEN(P[a],P[b])
    commute[a,b] ← THEN(P[a],P[b]) equals THEN(P[b],P[a])

COMMUTING-REDUCE(sequence):
    out ← []; pending ← reverse(sequence)
    while pending not empty:
        x ← pop(pending); j ← length(out)−1
        while j ≥ 0:
            if combine[out[j],x] exists: break
            if not commute[out[j],x]: j ← −1; break
            j ← j−1
        if j < 0: append x to out; continue
        replacement ← combine[out[j],x]
        tail ← out strictly after j; truncate out before j
        push tail onto pending in reverse order
        if replacement is a primitive: push replacement onto pending
    return out

ADD-GENERATOR(p,sequence):
    if sequence absent or length(sequence) > generatorLengthCap: return
    if key(p) already in pool:
        replace its word only if sequence is shorter; return
    if pool count reached generatorCountCap: return
    level ← first base index moved by p
    if p = I: return
    append {permutation:p,sequence,level} to pool
    index it by key(p)`)}
${code('IMPROVE: replace a transporter and sift the displaced one',`IMPROVE(p,sequence,start=0):
    for i = start,…,r−1:
        x ← p[B[i]]
        if x = B[i]: continue
        t ← representatives[i][x]
        require t exists in the certified orbit
        if t has no expanded sequence or length(sequence) < length(t.sequence):
            old ← t
            representatives[i][x] ← {p,sequence}
            add bounded generator (p,sequence) to candidate pool
            p ← old.permutation; sequence ← old.sequence
            if sequence absent: return
            t ← new representatives[i][x]
        if length(sequence)+length(t.sequence) > 3×candidateCap: return
        sequence ← COMMUTING-REDUCE(sequence ++ inverse(t.sequence))
        if sequence empty or too long: return
        p ← THEN(p,INVERSE(t.permutation))
        if length(sequence) ≤ residualLengthCap:
            add bounded generator (p,sequence) to candidate pool`)}
<p>The pool deduplicates full permutations and keeps shorter expressions when rediscovered. Seed it with primitives and available representatives, then all primitive pairs and commutators. Add deterministic pseudorandom trials alternating words of length 3–10 with conjugates of seeded commutators: chronological <code>[a,b,…commutator…,b⁻¹,a⁻¹]</code>. Each legal candidate goes through <code>IMPROVE</code>.</p>
<p>In each bounded pass, visit levels from deep to shallow. Use pooled generators fixing the earlier base points for weighted address Dijkstra; offer shorter resulting representatives to <code>IMPROVE</code>. Next, in descending generator-level order, try each generator after each existing representative at that level and sift those candidates. Stop after eight passes, a pass without improvements or the deadline.</p>
<p>Defaults are 5 seconds; candidate length 600; pooled generator length 100; residual length 18; at most 12,000 generators; 2,000 random seeds with seed 123456789. The refinement runner instead uses seed 20261012, 3,000 seeds and 4 seconds for degree below 90 or 20 seconds otherwise. These are heuristic bounds, not limits on the legal group. The optimizer requires an inverse primitive for every move; otherwise it returns the unchanged cache.</p>
<p>The acceptance gate evaluates changed DAG expressions, verifies their stored complete permutations, base-prefix fixation and destinations, and rejects any increase in representative word length. It also profiles 32 fixed legal scrambles, with depths cycling through 5, 10, 20, 30, 50 and 80. Accept only if some representative improves and the representative length bound, sample mean and sample 95th percentile do not worsen. Samples detect regressions; they do not prove globally shortest words or a universal average-case claim.</p>
<p>Cache sharing between puzzles first requires equal degrees, identical move arrays in identical order and equal bases. Import only shorter compatible representatives, offset signed DAG references, compact reachable nodes, then repeat exact verification and benchmark gating. Similar geometry or colors alone are insufficient.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-measured-refinement','Measured improvements and their limits',`
<p>The recorded refinement revisited the previous 34-puzzle catalog. Eleven accepted cache updates shorten 828 stored representatives while retaining complete orbit coverage and passing the same permutation/base-prefix checks. The deterministic benchmark uses 32 fixed scrambles per model at several depths: its total primitive count falls from 297,638 to 285,800, a 3.98% reduction. These samples measure the baseline constructive words; they are not a theorem that every scramble, every device, or every constrained request improves.</p>
<p>A separate constructed-request benchmark compares the old and enlarged catalogs. It finds 1,176 newly ready requests across 33 puzzles and 18 shorter ready answers among 1,249 tested extension cases. The cases were deliberately constructed from added legal operations, so this is a capability witness, not a representative success-rate estimate for arbitrary user selections.</p>
<p>For 816 lookup cases across that 34-puzzle catalog, indexed lookup inspects 1,792 operations instead of 229,998. On the measured run, lookup CPU time falls from 47.2 ms to 43.2 ms in aggregate; one-time index construction adds 137.1 ms across the entire catalog. This setup cost is amortized across repeated requests. Lower candidate counts are robust; exact elapsed times are hardware- and load-dependent, and individual small requests can be slower. The unchanged constructive and bounded-search fallbacks remain available.</p>
<p>Reproduction data are <code>solver-backend/data/full-solver/refinement-report.json</code>, <code>solver-backend/data/color-solver/conjugacy-report.json</code>, and <code>solver-backend/data/color-solver/index-benchmark.json</code>. All retained words still undergo independent playback verification. Faster lookup and more cached witnesses do not convert unknown requests into impossibility proofs or certify globally shortest words.</p>
`)}
${subsection('tech-shorten-format','Serialization, model signature and coverage verification',`
<p>A full cache contains <code>schemaVersion:1</code>, <code>status:"ready"</code>, model ID, degree, <code>modelSignature</code>, full base, levels, words, <code>complete:true</code> and statistics. <code>words[0]</code> is null. A leaf is <code>[moveIndex]</code>; concatenation is <code>[signedLeftRef,signedRightRef]</code>, with each absolute child reference preceding the parent. Each nonidentity level entry is <code>[destination,wordRef,permutationArray]</code>; the level-base identity entry is implicit.</p>
<p>Serialization traces words reachable from stored representatives, discards dead construction nodes and remaps signed references in topological order. The optimizer similarly compacts after replacements; its new-word builder locally reuses identical concatenation pairs. This does not require flattening the expressions.</p>
<p>The signature starts at 2166136261. Feed degree, move count and every move-array entry in order; for each integer <i>x</i>, compute <code>h = imul(h XOR x,16777619) unsigned32</code>. Encode eight hexadecimal digits. This lightweight compatibility check ignores descriptive metadata and is not collision-resistant security hashing. Independent audits additionally compare move arrays and cryptographic hashes of published assets.</p>
${code('VERIFY-CACHE',`require matching degree, signature and full base permutation
require complete=true and exactly r level tables
E[0] ← I
for ref = 1,…,wordNodeCount−1:
    if words[ref] is [j]: require legal index; E[ref] ← P[j]
    else (a,b) ← words[ref]:
        require abs(a),abs(b) < ref
        E[ref] ← THEN(SIGNED-EVAL(a),SIGNED-EVAL(b))
where SIGNED-EVAL(x) = E[x] if x≥0, otherwise INVERSE(E[−x])

product ← arbitrary-precision integer 1
for i = 0,…,r−1:
    require level[i].base = B[i]; seen ← {B[i]}
    for (destination,word,storedPermutation) in level[i].entries:
        require destination valid and not in seen; add it to seen
        p ← SIGNED-EVAL(word)
        require p = storedPermutation and p[B[i]] = destination
        require p[B[j]] = B[j] for every j<i
    product ← product × size(seen)
independentChain ← BUILD-CHAIN(original moves,B,unweighted)
require independentChain completed within its audit budget
independentOrder ← product of its orbit sizes, as exact big integers
require product = independentOrder
return complete coverage verified`)}
<p>Legal prefix-fixing representatives produce distinct legal transversal products. If their count equals the independently computed group order, they cover the group without enumerating its configurations. Check that published model/cache bytes are those audited. Legal-scramble replay and native-engine endpoint tests then check that this algebraic model matches displayed moves. Neither algebraic coverage alone nor a few successful scrambles establishes both facts.</p>
<p>Implementation sources are <code>solver-backend/src/group.cjs</code>, <code>full-solve.cjs</code>, <code>shorten.cjs</code> and tools <code>precompute-full.cjs</code>, <code>optimize-cache.cjs</code>, <code>refine-caches.cjs</code>, <code>audit-complete-coverage.cjs</code>. Browser bundling changes scheduling and delivery, not permutation identities. The runtime chapter specifies request routing, availability results and playback safeguards.</p>
`)}
`, 'Shorten words and improve valid transporters, then verify every witness and the cache’s complete group coverage.');
return {chain,full,shorten};
};
})();
